// The explanations of the codes of 0.26.2's text and syntax: `where` and
// argument-position `impl Trait` (front/parser/items/params.vl),
// visibility (`pub(crate)`, front/modules), format literals
// (front/check/format.vl); docs/grammar.md, "Items", "Modules",
// "Formatting". (0.26.3) f-strings removed: syntax.fstring
// (front/parser/exprs.vl); `#[deprecated]` (front/check/deprecated.vl).

use crate::tools::explain::Explain;

pub const TEXT_COUNT: i64 = 10;

pub static TEXT_TABLE: [Explain<'_>; 10] = [
    Explain {
        code: b"syntax.tilde", severity: b"error",
        means: b"(0.29) `~x`, the complement of an integer in C and in vole until 0.28. `~` is not an operator: `!x` is the bitwise not of an integer and the logical not of a bool, as in Rust (0.27.2 on read both spellings). The expression is read as `!x`, so its operand is checked too.",
        fix: b"Write `!x` (the error's fix: `volec fix --suggested FILE` rewrites every `~` of the program that is an operator).",
        example: b"fn main() {\n\
            \x20   let mask: u32 = ~0xffu32;\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:2:21: error[syntax.tilde] `~` is not an operator; an integer's complement is `!x`\n\
            \x20 help: write `!x`: bitwise not on an integer, logical not on a bool (as in Rust)\n\
            \x20 try: volec fix --suggested main.vl",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"syntax.default-arg", severity: b"error",
        means: b"(0.28.0) A parameter declared with a default value, `b: i64 = 2` (0.15 to 0.27). No parameter has one: every call passes every argument, by position or by name (`f(1, b: 3)`: named arguments stay, after the positional ones and in the parameters' order). The parameter is read without its value, so a call that left it out is reported too (`missing argument 'b'`).",
        fix: b"Remove the `= value` (the error's fix: `volec fix --suggested FILE`), then pass the value at the calls that left it out: each one's error gives the call to write. To add a new parameter and give every call its value in one step: `volec refactor signature FN --add 'x: T = VALUE'`. A function that is called two ways reads better as two functions (std::gpu's `write_texture` and `write_texture_region`).",
        example: b"fn f(a: i64, b: i64 = 2) -> i64 { a + b }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:1:21: error[syntax.default-arg] a parameter has no default value; pass it at each call\n\
            \x20 help: remove the `= value` and give the argument where the function is called (a named argument, `f(1, b: 3)`, is still written)",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"item.private", severity: b"error",
        means: b"(0.26.2) An item (a function, a type, a constant, a static, a module) used outside the module that declares it, where it is not visible (Rust's E0603): an item not marked `pub` is private to its module and the modules inside it; one marked `pub(crate)`, `pub(super)` or `pub(in path)` is visible only up to that module (the message says which: `crate 'std'`, `module 'a::b'`). Until 0.26.2 this was a `type error`.",
        fix: b"In your own program, mark it `pub` where it is declared, or widen its restriction (the help gives the declaration; `volec fix --only pub` writes `pub` for a private one and `pub(crate)` for a restricted one). Std's `pub(crate)` items are std's own: use its public API.",
        example: b"error[item.private] 'super_fn' is visible only in module 'outer' | help: make it visible in its crate: `pub(crate) fn super_fn` at main.vl:3:20",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"vis.path", severity: b"error",
        means: b"(0.26.2) `pub(in path)` whose path names no module, or a module that is not the item's own or one around it (Rust's E0742); or `pub(super)` at a crate's root, which has no parent in the crate. The path starts at `crate`, `self`, `super` (in std, `std::` or `super::`). The item is then visible in its crate.",
        fix: b"Name the item's module or one of its ancestors: `pub(in crate::a)` for an item of `a::b`, or write `pub(super)` or `pub(crate)`.",
        example: b"error[vis.path] 'pub(in crate::outer::b)': the path names a module that is not around this item | help: 'pub(in path)' names the item's module or one around it: 'pub(in crate::a)', 'pub(super)', 'pub(crate)'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"format.syntax",
        severity: b"error",
        means: b"(0.26.2) A format literal (a plain string literal that is the first argument of println, \
            print, eprintln, eprint, format_in, format_args, write, writeln, panic, the message of \
            assert and debug_assert, or a contract's `else`) whose braces do not make holes: a `{` \
            never closed, a `}` with no `{`. Its grammar is Rust's std::fmt: `{}`, `{0}`, `{name}`, \
            each with an optional `:spec`, and `{{` and `}}` for a brace of the text.",
        fix: b"Double the braces of the text: `println(\"{{\")` prints `{` (`volec fix --suggested` \
            writes it).",
        example: b"fn main() {\n\
            \x20   println(\"done }\");\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:2:19: error[format.syntax] unmatched '}' in a format string\n\
            \x20 help: a '}' of the text is written '}}'\n\
            \x20 try: volec fix --suggested main.vl",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"format.spec", severity: b"error",
        means: b"(0.26.2) A hole's spec, after its `:`, that is not Rust's: `[[fill]align][sign]['#']['0'][width]['.' precision][type]`, align `<` `^` `>`, sign `+`, width and precision an integer, `N$` or `name$` (precision also `*`), type `?` `x?` `X?` `x` `X` `o` `b` `e` `E` `p`, and vole's `c` (a u8 as its byte). `{:#?}`, Rust's Debug over several lines, is not in this release.",
        fix: b"Write the spec in Rust's order: `{x:>8.2}`, `{n:#010x}`, `{s:*^9}`, `{v:?}`; for `{:#?}`, `{:?}`.",
        example: b"error[format.spec] invalid format spec ':q': unknown format trait 'q' | help: a spec is [[fill]align][+][#][0][width][.precision][type]: align < ^ >, width and precision N, N$ or name$ (precision also *), type ? x? X? x X o b e E p c: '{x:>8.2}', '{v:?}', '{n:#010x}', '{s:*^9}'",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"format.args", severity: b"error",
        means: b"(0.26.2) A format call's arguments do not match its literal's holes (Rust's \"argument never used\", \"invalid reference\"): an argument (positional or named, `name = expr`) that no hole takes, a hole (`{}`, `{N}`, `N$`, `.*`) with no argument for it; or arguments after a first argument that is not a literal (`println(s, x)`): a format string is a literal, a `const` or a variable is printed as a value.",
        fix: b"Give each hole its argument, after the literal and in order, or remove the argument; for a variable format, format the pieces apart (`print(s); println(\"{}\", x)`) or build the value with `format_args(\"..\", x)`.",
        example: b"error[format.args] this argument is never used: no hole of the format string takes it | help: write a hole for it, '{}', or remove it",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"format.expr-hole",
        severity: b"error",
        means: b"(0.26.2) A hole of a format literal holding an expression, `{p.a}`, `{x + 1}`, \
            `{v.len()}`: a hole names a local, a parameter, a `const` or a `static` (Rust 2021's \
            inline captures), or takes the next argument.",
        fix: b"Write `{}` and pass the expression after the literal: `println(\"{}\", p.a)` (`volec fix \
            --suggested` moves it).",
        example: b"struct P { a: i64 }\n\
            \n\
            fn main() {\n\
            \x20   let p = P { a: 1 };\n\
            \x20   println(\"a = {p.a}\");\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:5:19: error[format.expr-hole] '{p.a}': a hole of a format string holds a name, not an expression\n\
            \x20 help: write '{}' and pass the expression after the format string, as Rust does: 'println(\"{} {x}\", a.b)'\n\
            \x20 try: volec fix --suggested main.vl",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"syntax.fstring",
        severity: b"error (from 0.26.3)",
        means: b"An f-string, `f\"x = {x}, len {v.len()}\"` (0.15 to 0.26.2), is removed: a format string \
            is Rust's, a plain literal that is the first argument of println, print, eprintln, \
            format_in, panic, write, writeln, a contract's `else` or format_args (0.26.2). A hole \
            names a local, a parameter, a const or a static, or takes the next argument, `{}`; an \
            expression is an argument after the literal. The error comes once per file, at its first \
            f-string, with how many the file has.",
        fix: b"Write the plain literal: a name hole stays, another hole becomes `{}` (`{:spec}`) and \
            its expression an argument after the literal, in order.",
        example: b"fn main() {\n\
            \x20   let v = [1, 2, 3];\n\
            \x20   let n = 3;\n\
            \x20   println(f\"n = {n}, sum {v[0] + v[1]}\");   // now: println(\"n = {n}, sum {}\", v[0] + v[1]);\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:4:13: error[syntax.fstring] f-strings are removed (0.26.3): a format string takes the values as arguments\n\
            \x20 help: a name stays in its hole and an expression goes after the literal: `println(\"{n} {}\", v.len())`; elsewhere `format_args(\"..\", args)`",
    },
    Explain {
        code: b"item.deprecated",
        severity: b"warning",
        means: b"(0.26.3) A use of an item marked `#[deprecated]` (Rust's attribute: `#[deprecated]`, \
            `#[deprecated = \"note\"]`, `#[deprecated(since = \"1.2\", note = \"use new_fn\")]`), on \
            a function, a method, a struct, an enum, a trait, a type alias, a constant, a static or \
            a field: a call, a name, a type, a path, a `use`, a field read or a struct literal's \
            field. The message gives the item's note; the help, where it is marked. A use inside a \
            deprecated item (a recursive call, a derived impl, a deprecated method reading a \
            deprecated field) is not one, as in Rust, and std's uses of its own deprecated items are \
            std's.",
        fix: b"Do what the note says (it names what to use instead).",
        example: b"#[deprecated(note = \"use new_fn\")]\n\
            fn old_fn() -> i64 { 1 }\n\
            \n\
            fn new_fn() -> i64 { 1 }\n\
            \n\
            fn main() {\n\
            \x20   println(\"{} {}\", old_fn(), new_fn());\n\
            }\n\
            \n\
            main.vl:7:22: warning[item.deprecated] use of deprecated function 'old_fn': use new_fn\n\
            \x20 help: marked #[deprecated] at main.vl:2:1",
    },
];
